WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest is
a legendary award-winning

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.604
best-selling comic-book writer.

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:08.174
Their debut novel, "Luda,"
is out now.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:11.110
Please welcome back to the show
one of our favorites,

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.679
Grant Morrison, everybody!

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.447
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:21.020 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:27.593 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.028
-Welcome back
to the show, Grant.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:30.529
-Thanks for inviting me back.
It's great to be here again.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:31.564
-I'm thrilled to have you.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.099
I want to talk
about your writing.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.767
I also want to take
this opportunity.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:38.270
Obviously a very sad day,
you being Scottish.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.706
Are you processing --

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:43.209
How are you processing
the passing of Queen Elizabeth?

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.078
-I heard about it just like
an hour ago, and it's kind of --

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.147
It's going to be
a major existential shock

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:49.515
for our country.

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:51.417
But for me,
it's the passing of an age.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.219
That was the second
Elizabethan age.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.954
We had the explosion
of creativity

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:57.356
that gave us the Beatles
and Brit rock

00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:59.192
and British artists
and British writers.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.827
And I think
it's just really weird.

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.328
That's the end of that now.

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.098
It's like the Shakespearean age
is over, you know,

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:08.468
except it's now,
so it's a weird one.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:11.637
We Scots have a complicated
relationship with the monarchy,

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:13.940
so it's an interesting one,
I think, a bit.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.475
-Yeah, I think it will take
all of us a bit of time.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.511
The loss of a constant
is a very shocking thing.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.480
-Yeah, absolutely.
-When there's so much upheaval,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.215
you realize, oh, that was --
the length of time.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:25.552
-And I think they should
just jump to the kid, honestly.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:27.587
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.288
-Now we're getting
to the nitty-gritty.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:32.158
-The serious stuff. You know?

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:36.696
-So, you're beloved. You're
a well-known comic-book writer.

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:38.564
And, yet, this is
your first novel.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:41.034
What was, one, the decision
to write the novel?

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:43.869
And, two, how different
was the process?

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.771
-Well, basically, my agent
came to me and said,

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:46.939
"Do you want to write a novel?"

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:48.274
And I've never managed
to do one.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.842
Because I like
books with pictures.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.477
You know, I'm basic like that.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.512
And so if it hasn't
got a drawing,

00:01:53.512 --> 00:01:55.315
then I'm not interested.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.517
But I kind of figured,
that was the difference.

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.919
You know, I wasn't working with
a collaborator this time.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.254
I'm used to having an artist.

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:02.755
Or if I worked on television,
as you know,

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.357
there's producers,
there's writers rooms.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.526
It's a big thing.
It's like being in a band

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.394
or being in a football team.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:11.397
So it was weird
to just be sitting on my own

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.232
and I had to make
the pictures into words.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:15.334
And that was fun
because COVID had happened.

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.803
So there's nowhere to go.
I was kind of stuck in the room.

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.706
And a book came out of it.

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:23.142
It would never happened
but for the virus.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.311
-Don't brag that up too much.
-No, certainly.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.413
-Those people were like,
"Did you write a book, too?"

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.981
It's like, "No."

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:32.418
-I think most people tried.
-Most tried.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.387
Well, it's very impressive
that you got it done.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:38.758
This is a story
about an aging drag queen

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.793
and a younger sort of prodigy

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:43.329
who comes into their life
and how it affects it.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.998
Where did this --
the seed of that idea come from?

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:47.366
-The idea came from --

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.469
I wanted to do a retelling
of the old story of

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.171
Merlin the magician,
the Arthurian legend.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.475
And he had this story where
Merlin at the end of his life,

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:58.011
he meets this young witch
called Vivian.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.646
And she flatters him and she
kind of wants to learn magic.

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:02.348
And, of course,
it all goes badly.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.317
You know, he's just the old goat
who thinks

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.251
this beautiful young woman
is interested in him.

00:03:06.251 --> 00:03:07.719
And he teaches her
all the secrets,

00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:10.189
and then she locks him in
an oak tree for all eternity.

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.892
So a lesson for us all, I think.

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.927
-Don't trust the young.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:16.929
-Yeah, do not trust the young,

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:19.732
especially the young
who want to learn your secrets.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:22.435
So it started from that.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:23.970
And I thought that's a story

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.571
that really needs
a drag makeover.

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.540
I want to see Merlin in a dress.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.309
So the idea of doing
a contemporary version of that

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:33.346
and set in this theater
world that was super-contained

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:36.649
with these characters who were
basically living in a world,

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:38.618
which is our world, where we
can't tell the difference

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.319
between reality
and illusion anymore.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:44.156
We're in the world of fake news
and VR and AR, all that stuff.

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:46.359
So this book is kind of,
how do you negotiate

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:47.793
what we don't know what's real

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.996
and what's true
and what's fake?

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:53.232
-To some degree,
it seems like drag

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:56.135
is a huge departure
from, say, superheroes.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.271
But in another way,
it's not at all.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:03:59.572
-Well, absolutely.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.206
You think of Superman,
Peter Parker,

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:02.875
those are just nerdy guys,

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:05.344
but then they dress up in these
super flamboyant costumes

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:07.880
and go out at night?

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.382
Batman's the worst.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:12.451
Batman's dressed
for the fetish club.

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:13.920
He gets in his big car,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.955
and he cruises
the streets of Gotham.

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:19.158
He's obviously -- He's hoping
the Joker notices.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.494
You know, he wants that.
He wants to be seen.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:24.229
Laughter ]
And that's why.

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:26.265
There's no way he's going out
as Bruce Wayne.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.600
That guy's putting on
the ears...and the cape

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.171
and is like, "Notice me!"

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:35.241
So I think it's just
a hop, skip, and a jump

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:38.077
from RuPaul to Bruce Wayne,
you know?

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:41.680
-And with each passing day,
RuPaul almost has as much money

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.916
as Bruce Wayne, so that --
It's getting closer.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.751
-Yeah, and I know
she fights crime on the side.

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:48.554
-Yeah. And you --

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.790
Do you feel like you've had
your own experiences with drag?

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.224
-Absolutely.

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:55.027
When I was 11 years old,
it kind of started for me.

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.230
And I remember
there was this British comic

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.497
and there was this character

00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:00.399
called Danny Merlin,
son of the wise.

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:02.368
And the guy was a magician,
this boy magician.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.870
Harry Potter for weird dudes.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.507
And he wore
this mini-toga thing.

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:08.875
It was a really unusual costume.

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:10.376
Bare legs and a pair of sandals.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.411
So I thought,
"I'll dress up as this guy."

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:15.047
I love the adventures
of Danny Merlin.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:16.749
So, I get dressed up
to be this boy magician,

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:19.118
got myself in a vest,
pulled it down, got my --

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:20.886
Mom's belt on.

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:22.421
And then I looked in the mirror
and I thought,

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:24.557
"Holy...you look great."

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:26.358
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:28.527
And I thought, "My God,
I look like one of these models

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:30.596
in the 'Vogue' magazines
my mom's got lying about."

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.865
So it kind of messed
with my head.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:35.634
And in that moment,
drag, comics, and magic

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:40.172
all hit me like a lightning bolt
and came about through this.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:43.141
Yeah, I did that
for years on and off.

00:05:43.141 --> 00:05:45.644
I was interested in performance
more than anything to do

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:47.113
with gender or sexuality.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:49.749
It was all about performance
and letting loose

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:52.785
all these different characters
that I felt was inside.

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:55.887
-This book takes place
in "Gasglow,"

00:05:55.887 --> 00:05:57.556
which is a version --

00:05:57.556 --> 00:06:00.360
I guess sort of
a different universe version

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.563
of Glasgow, where you're from.
-Yeah.

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:05.631
-Have you had that name,
that version of

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:06.899
Glasgow in your head forever?

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:08.333
Because it's almost too good
to be true.

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.203
-Yeah, I didn't have the name,
but I had this version --

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.904
I had a dream
where there was a giant map

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.739
of a much bigger version
of the city,

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.808
and it was the city
that I visit in dreams.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.577
You know, I think a lot of us
have got a version of a hometown

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:20.379
or a city
that we go to in dreams

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.482
and it's got places
that once existed

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:23.850
or places that don't exist.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.751
But it was pretty consistent
for me.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:28.087
And out of that came
the notion of Gasglow.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.723
But it was a combination
of my hometown

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:33.125
and Glasgow and Brooklyn,
because they're really similar.

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:34.660
You know,
when I got to New York,

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.730
it was like -- this is like
Glasgow but gigantic.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.431
Because Glasgow's
got the grid systems

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:41.367
and it's got the kind of energy
and the violence.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:44.136
And every band in Glasgow
used to want to be --

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:46.639
They all wanted to be
the Velvet Underground.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:49.041
So New York was kind of
a big influence.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.977
So Gasglow is really --
it's why the character

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:53.712
talks in both British
and American idioms.

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:55.414
Because I wanted to
smash those two together

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:58.383
and kind of create this -- as
you say -- a kind of dream city

00:06:58.383 --> 00:06:59.952
that combined elements of both.

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:03.022
-You dabbled in some punk music
back in the day.

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.356
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:07:04.356 --> 00:07:06.125
-Were you -- How did it go?
-It went pretty well.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:07.826
We had a pretty good band.

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:09.895
But we all fell out in the end

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.630
because, you know,
bands always fall out.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.966
And then there's a little bit
of that in the book, as well,

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:15.867
looking at the mad dynamics
that happen

00:07:15.867 --> 00:07:17.336
in a group of people.

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.138
But, yeah, we had a good time.
We were psychedelic punks.

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:20.839
That's what we thought
of ourselves.

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:22.174
We were really into punk music,

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.010
but we loved to dress
like the Rolling Stones

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:27.046
and doing all this
kind of flamboyant frock coats

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:29.214
and feathers
and stuff like that.

00:07:29.214 --> 00:07:31.016
So we would play
this crazy fast music.

00:07:31.016 --> 00:07:32.985
But we were part
of the whole scene

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:35.621
that became Creation Records
and Alan McGee

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.657
with The Jesus and Mary chain
and Primal Scream.

00:07:37.657 --> 00:07:41.193
So we used to support those guys
and we knew them pretty well.

00:07:41.193 --> 00:07:43.295
-That must've been
a fascinating time to be there.

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:44.997
-Yeah, it was
a kind of wild time.

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:47.399
And so much of that influenced
things like Nirvana.

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:48.968
And, you know, they were
really influenced by those

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.404
Glasgow indie bands of the time.

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:55.708
-Now, you know, obviously,
music is in the rear view

00:07:55.708 --> 00:07:59.311
and you've become this
sort of authority on comics.

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:02.448
And I have heard that people
often ask you questions

00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:05.084
about comic-book superheroes

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.720
that maybe you weren't
prepared to answer.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.122
-Well, always, you know, people
have got these wild ideas.

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:11.924
It's like they want to know
because the kids

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.893
who are into this stuff,
they take it super seriously.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:16.529
So they want to know who pumps
the tires in the Batmobile.

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.830
Surely it's not him.
Does Alfred do it?

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.533
You know, Alfred's too busy
making sandwiches.

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:22.368
So who the hell
is pumping the tires?

00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:24.269
Who's putting the gas
in the Batmobile?

00:08:24.269 --> 00:08:29.007
It doesn't pull up to a station
like, "Yeah, just fill it up."

00:08:29.007 --> 00:08:30.476
So, questions like that.

00:08:30.476 --> 00:08:33.979
And the one I loved most was,
does Superman eat?

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:36.215
Because -- Does he have to eat?
Superman.

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.949
He's invulnerable.
He can do anything.

00:08:37.949 --> 00:08:39.285
Surely, he doesn't have to eat.

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:41.153
And I'm having to think
of this question.

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:43.155
And I spoke to the kid,
and I said,

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:45.524
"I think he does eat,
but he's Superman,

00:08:45.524 --> 00:08:48.294
so he takes every iota of energy
from anything he eats

00:08:48.294 --> 00:08:50.129
so there's no waste product."

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:54.734
And then the guy says, "So,
he doesn't go to the bathroom?

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:58.637
So does it seal up then
over time?"

00:08:58.637 --> 00:09:01.740
And I'm like,
"You're leading me into a place

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.275
I just don't want to go."

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:05.744
I'm thinking of
Superman flying through the air

00:09:05.744 --> 00:09:07.246
with his ass sealing up?

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:10.015
I mean, give me a break here.

00:09:10.015 --> 00:09:12.417
So, questions like that.
And the worst ones are,

00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:15.321
"Can you explain the universe
and God to me?"

00:09:15.321 --> 00:09:16.922
People have been
trying to do this

00:09:16.922 --> 00:09:18.690
for the last 6,000 years
of human history,

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.559
and you've come to me?

00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:22.661
This preposterous figure
in a blue suit

00:09:22.661 --> 00:09:24.696
and asking me about God
and the universe?

00:09:24.696 --> 00:09:26.999
But I always try, and
I always give them an answer

00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:28.467
that I hope maybe
will send them away

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:30.869
with a prayer in their heart
and a song in their head.

00:09:30.869 --> 00:09:33.505
-That's very sweet.
Another really sweet thing --

00:09:33.505 --> 00:09:37.076
And I just did this for my son
because he wrote,

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:40.145
you know, drawings
of Green Lantern and Aquaman,

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:41.847
and we always
staple it together.

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:44.349
But your mom went and printed
your first comics.

00:09:44.349 --> 00:09:45.851
-She did, yeah.

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:47.586
She used to take them into
the office and print them out

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.588
on one of those
super old-school photo copiers

00:09:49.588 --> 00:09:51.290
where everything was faded
by the time

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.125
they got to the last page.
-Yeah.

00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:55.061
-And she would do --
just churn these things out.

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:56.762
And her friends at work
just used to be,

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:58.096
"What the hell is this?

00:09:58.096 --> 00:09:59.765
You should be working.
You shouldn't be doing this."

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:01.466
But yeah, she would do,
like, 300 copies

00:10:01.466 --> 00:10:03.135
of some ridiculous story
that I'd done.

00:10:03.135 --> 00:10:04.903
And then I could take them in
and they looked --

00:10:04.903 --> 00:10:08.274
...I'm really published.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:11.243
"No, actually, my mom
printed it up last night."

00:10:11.243 --> 00:10:14.446
-We should all be so lucky
to have that support system.

00:10:14.446 --> 00:10:16.548
And thanks for being here.

00:10:16.548 --> 00:10:18.183
-It's always great
to talk to you.

00:10:18.183 --> 00:10:19.852
-Grant Morrison.
"Luda" is out now.

00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:22.254
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

